WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.320
- [Narrator] Ketchup,
it's everywhere in the US.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.310
97% of Americans have a
bottle in their fridge.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:11.730
It's the sauce we put on our
hamburgers, our hot dogs,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.540
and our french fries.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.208
But the story of ketchup
actually begins in Asia.

00:00:17.208 --> 00:00:21.910
(bottle shattering and quiet music)

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.410
We think of ketchup as a thick red sauce,

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.920
but it was something pretty
different in the beginning.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.750
It originated as a thin soy
sauce made from fermented fish

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.330
most likely from a region called Tonkin,

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.890
or in what we call Vietnam today.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.760
It was common throughout Southeast
Asia in the 17th century.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.470
Ketchup was called kêtsiap, a Chinese word

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.780
from the Amoy dialect that translates

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.850
to "brine of pickled fish."

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.309
- I look at that and
say, how is that possible

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:55.930
that this little product
that starts in Indonesia

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.610
goes to UK, comes to the US,
and then, all of a sudden

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:01.970
spread across this world.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.780
The British had a colony
in what is today Indonesia,

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.400
and it is there that they first
ran into the word kêtsiap,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.438
which meant to them soy sauce.

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:15.250
Many other people visited them,

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.980
they fell in love with soy sauce,

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:22.060
and they liked to take
the idea back to England.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.483
The problem was there were no soybeans

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:26.090
growing in England at the time,

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.340
so they began to experiment.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.060
Rather than soybeans,
"let's do mushrooms."

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.130
So they had mushroom ketchup.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.180
"Let's do fish." And so
they had fish ketchup.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.860
And they said, "Let's do beans,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.390
so let's have bean ketchup."

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:44.210
So it became a long series of products

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.940
that did not include tomatoes.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.710
So it really was something that
was common and did not have

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.900
a specific meaning other
than it was a main product

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:53.893
that was spiced.

00:01:54.973 --> 00:01:55.860
- [Narrator] There are no
rules for how the spell

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.970
the word "ketchup" or for what defines it,

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.910
so cooks experimented with
a variety of ingredients

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.290
to season meat, fish, bread,
whatever needed flavor.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.220
Andrew Smith's book, "Pure Ketchup,"

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.843
contains 50 different
historical ketchup recipes

00:02:11.843 --> 00:02:15.940
including Eliza Smith's 1727 recipe

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.860
which was the first one
published in English.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.470
Some of the listed ingredients
are anchovies, shallots,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.540
white wine vinegar, white
wine, mace, ginger, cloves,

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.250
peppers, a nutmeg, a lemon
peel, and horseradish.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.123
So what happened to all
those varieties of ketchup?

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:36.720
Tomatoes.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.090
The 1812 recipe from James
Mease is the first appearance

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.620
of tomatoes in ketchup.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.400
But wait, what about Heinz,
the ketchup we know and love?

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.640
- H.J. Heinz was in the
right place at the right time

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.040
with the right product.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.260
So in one sense, it was just pure luck

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.150
that he had a good product
at the time that french fries

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.950
came in, at the time
that hot dogs came in,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.400
at the time that hamburgers came in.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.427
And it very quickly took over the market

00:03:02.427 --> 00:03:05.450
and it has dominated the
market for the last 100 years.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:06.840
- [Narrator] While tomato-based ketchup

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.050
is the most common now, there
are still specialty versions

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.773
with spinach, carrots
and butternut squash,

00:03:12.773 --> 00:03:17.520
cinnamon and cloves,
jalapenos, Vindaloo spices,

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.680
bacon, and truffles.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.144
So the next time you grab
that bottle of ketchup,

00:03:22.144 --> 00:03:25.290
remember it wasn't always tomato-based,

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.090
and it traveled the world
before it go to you.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:28.923
- Oh, look at that.

00:03:28.923 --> 00:03:30.745
It sticks. It doesn't drip off.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.700
Let me have another one here to make sure

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.067
the quality is as good as it should be.

